00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:03.870
A useful feature within Windows
is the ability to compress files

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.460
and folders to save disk space.

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:10.310
This isn't as much of an issue as it is
to be as, most hard drives have a large

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:14.709
capacity, there are a large capacity
flash drives and also cloud storage.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:19.850
However you may still wish to send a
file or a folder to an email recipient

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:25.640
or archive it and compressing the file
or folder will save some disk space.

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:28.660
So just to demonstrate how
that can be done, I'm going to

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.810
use the WP2 assessment folder.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:34.680
Now, if I move my mouse over that,
and you can see that the information

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:37.830
is that it's 322 kilobytes in size.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.870
So the files within
that are 322 kilobytes.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:44.490
What I'm going to do is I'm going
to compress that by right-clicking

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:50.650
on the folder and then select Send
To, and then when I select Send To I

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:55.900
click on compressed zipped folder and
that will then zip that folder up.

00:00:56.985 --> 00:01:00.975
So as you can see it has basically
made a duplicate of that folder,

00:01:01.185 --> 00:01:03.795
and it is identified with a
little zipper down the middle.

00:01:03.795 --> 00:01:06.705
Now as you can see, I've hovered
my mouse over it, and it's telling

00:01:06.705 --> 00:01:09.345
me that it's 172 kilobytes in size.

00:01:09.585 --> 00:01:13.515
So it has reduced the size of
the files within there by half.

00:01:13.965 --> 00:01:17.205
So, while that doesn't seem very large
it could be a case of that you could

00:01:17.205 --> 00:01:21.915
have a folder that contained information
that is five, 10, 15, 20 megabytes, and

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:26.005
then that can be reduced, which would
then make it easier to attach and save

00:01:26.005 --> 00:01:27.645
disk space if you are archiving it.

